To place an SVG icon (like a magnifying glass) on the right side of an input field and make it clickable, you can use a combination of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Here's a complete example:

### HTML
```html
<div class="search-container">
  <input type="text" id="search-input" placeholder="Search...">
  <button id="search-button" class="search-icon" onclick="handleSearch()">
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
      <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
      <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
    </svg>
  </button>
</div>
```

### CSS
```css
.search-container {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
  width: 300px; /* Adjust as needed */
}

#search-input {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px 40px 10px 10px; /* Right padding to make space for the icon */
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  border-radius: 4px;
  font-size: 16px;
}

.search-icon {
  position: absolute;
  right: 10px;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  background: none;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 5px;
}

.search-icon svg {
  pointer-events: none; /* Ensures clicks go through to the button */
}
```

### JavaScript
```javascript
function handleSearch() {
  const searchTerm = document.getElementById('search-input').value;
  console.log('Searching for:', searchTerm);
  // Add your search logic here
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **HTML Structure**:
   - The input and button are wrapped in a container with `position: relative`.
   - The button contains the SVG icon and has an `onclick` handler.

2. **CSS Styling**:
   - The input has right padding to prevent text from being hidden behind the icon.
   - The button is absolutely positioned on the right side of the input.
   - The SVG icon is styled to be clickable (via the button).

3. **JavaScript**:
   - The `handleSearch` function is called when the icon is clicked, and it retrieves the input value.

### Alternative (Using `addEventListener`):
If you prefer not to use inline JavaScript, you can attach the event listener in your script:

```javascript
document.getElementById('search-button').addEventListener('click', handleSearch);

function handleSearch() {
  const searchTerm = document.getElementById('search-input').value;
  console.log('Searching for:', searchTerm);
  // Your search logic here
}
```

This approach keeps your HTML clean and separates the behavior from the structure.

